I think there are four areas in which  people make mistakes on applying to the  University of Oxford the first one is  not applying now it's the only sure way  they are not going to get into the  university is if you don't send an  application in so if you think you've  got the academic merit and potential to  get into university and still well here  you must send an application the second  mistake is not practicing for the  admissions tests admissions tests might  kind of surprise lots of you but many  courses at Oxford have admissions tests  the reason is we have admissions testers  so that we have a level playing field  when looking at candidates their tests  that the university is set and it means  that we're looking at everybody the same  set of data in the same way that you  wouldn't sit your driving test your  a-levels or your GCC's without at least  considering how long it's going to take  you to set the paper to go through some  model answers and to see what style of  answer you should be submitting you  really shouldn't be sitting the  admissions test up doing a dry run  before and even if that's just at home  doing the answers yourself and then  looking through the the mark schemes  make sure you get on to the website and  try and find some previous pass papers  just to test them through before you  come up and sit the admissions tests in  November third mistake is what I'd call  not taking risks now this is a personal  application whether it's the personal  statement or the interview which is face  to face this is about things that  interest you now it's really good if you  can apply things to the course you be  studying at Oxford but there's no danger  in looking around as a subject it's  called wider reading don't worry about  delving into work that's slightly away  from the courses that you studied in  school but sort of touch some courses  might be doing that Oxford if you were  to get into the University you will be  studying a range of things around your  subject it's it's really important that  if you find something interesting and  you think it's slightly risky as long as  you're confident in following it through  do take that risk because it will pay  off in some ways for pharoah that people  make in their applications is being  scared of saying I don't know in the  interview now you mustn't use it too  much obviously oh it's better to have a  baseline knowledge of the tasks but you  must remember that the odds  view is not a knowledge test is about  thought processes so instead of saying I  don't know cold-cut if you add the word  but and relate to something that you do  know you're able to thank God the  cheaters through your put your thought  processes your being honest and upfront  without throwing in a load of rubbish  about something that you you vaguely  know about and actually just to see to  put something into the mix that will  guide you through the next part of the  process in whichever problem you might  be solving as part of the Oxford  interview so those are four common  mistakes that people make when applying  to Oxford not applying not practicing  the admissions tests not taking risks  I'm remembering it saying I don't know  is legitimate as long as you can take it  on to the next step of the interview  thank you so much to Dan for agreeing to  be interviewed if you'd like to check  out more videos about Oxford and  Cambridge admissions then check out the  playlist that's on the screen now and  linked in the description below also  take a look at the social media links in  the description for sant Peter's College  which is my old college there are links  to Facebook Twitter snapchat and  Instagram where you can see a snapshot  of student life at an Oxford College
 

